                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 905,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 886,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Takes the inverse of t.rfft: turns a half spectrum back into real samples.\n\nt.rfft keeps only the non-negative frequencies of a real signal; t.irfft rebuilds the samples from them, filling in the mirror half itself, so the result is REAL - no t.real needed. Go there and back to work on a signal in frequency terms: zero the bins above a cut-off for a brick-wall low pass, scale bins to reshape the tone, or multiply spectra to convolve.\n\nLENGTH:\nm frequency values come back as 2 x (m - 1) samples. 129 values (from 256 samples) give 256 again. An ODD number of samples does not survive the trip: 255 samples make 128 values, which come back as 254. Keep frame lengths even.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.irfft <dim: int>\nWith no argument it transforms along the last dimension.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.irfft\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA half spectrum, as t.rfft makes it: complex, n/2 + 1 values. Each tensor that arrives is transformed and sent at once. The imaginary parts of the first value (and, for an even length, the last) cannot be represented in a real signal and are ignored.\n\ndim (an int box, only when you give an argument):\nt.irfft 0 transforms along dimension 0 instead of the last one. With no argument the node has no dim box and always uses the last dimension, which is what you want for one signal, or for a stack of signals one per row.\n\nnorm (menu):\nWhere the 1/n scaling goes. 'backward' (the default) leaves the forward transform unscaled and divides on the way back, so a sine of size 1 over 256 samples makes a peak of 128. 'forward' divides on the way forward (that peak becomes 0.5 - half the sine's size, the other half sits in the mirror bin). 'ortho' divides both ways by the square root of n. A forward and an inverse node must use the same setting or the round trip comes back the wrong size.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nfft tensor out:\nA REAL tensor of 2 x (m - 1) samples along the transformed dimension.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe top plot is the signal going in; the bottom one is what comes back after t.rfft and t.irfft - the same 256 samples. Drag the slider and the two stay identical.\n\nRELATED:\nt.rfft is the forward transform. t.ifft is the inverse for the full spectrum t.fft makes, and returns complex samples.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 870,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Takes the inverse of t.rfft: turns a half spectrum back into real samples.\n\nt.rfft keeps only the non-negative frequencies of a real signal; t.irfft rebuilds the samples from them, filling in the mirror half itself, so the result is REAL - no t.real needed. Go there and back to work on a signal in frequency terms: zero the bins above a cut-off for a brick-wall low pass, scale bins to reshape the tone, or multiply spectra to convolve.\n\nLENGTH:\nm frequency values come back as 2 x (m - 1) samples. 129 values (from 256 samples) give 256 again. An ODD number of samples does not survive the trip: 255 samples make 128 values, which come back as 254. Keep frame lengths even.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.irfft <dim: int>\nWith no argument it transforms along the last dimension.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.irfft\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA half spectrum, as t.rfft makes it: complex, n/2 + 1 values. Each tensor that arrives is transformed and sent at once. The imaginary parts of the first value (and, for an even length, the last) cannot be represented in a real signal and are ignored.\n\ndim (an int box, only when you give an argument):\nt.irfft 0 transforms along dimension 0 instead of the last one. With no argument the node has no dim box and always uses the last dimension, which is what you want for one signal, or for a stack of signals one per row.\n\nnorm (menu):\nWhere the 1/n scaling goes. 'backward' (the default) leaves the forward transform unscaled and divides on the way back, so a sine of size 1 over 256 samples makes a peak of 128. 'forward' divides on the way forward (that peak becomes 0.5 - half the sine's size, the other half sits in the mirror bin). 'ortho' divides both ways by the square root of n. A forward and an inverse node must use the same setting or the round trip comes back the wrong size.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nfft tensor out:\nA REAL tensor of 2 x (m - 1) samples along the transformed dimension.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe top plot is the signal going in; the bottom one is what comes back after t.rfft and t.irfft - the same 256 samples. Drag the slider and the two stay identical.\n\nRELATED:\nt.rfft is the forward transform. t.ifft is the inverse for the full spectrum t.fft makes, and returns complex samples."
                },
